<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<tNVSE>
  <fonts>
    <!--
      Requirements: bEnableMultibyteFontHook=1 and
                    bEnableFreeTypeFontRendering=1 in tnvse.ini.
      Only font IDs listed in this file are handled by FreeType. Unlisted IDs
      continue to use the original .fnt/.tex renderer. If an ID is configured
      more than once, the last configuration replaces earlier ones.

      1. <font> attributes

      id
        Required positive integer identifying the game font slot. Common
        Fallout: New Vegas font slots are:
        1 = Glow_Monofonto_Large
        2 = Monofonto_Large
        3 = Glow_Monofonto_Medium
        4 = Monofonto_VeryLarge02_Dialogs2
        5 = Fixedsys_Comp_uniform_width (terminal font)
        6 = Glow_Monofonto_VL_dialogs
        7 = Baked-in_Monofonto_Large
        8 = Glow_Futura_Caps_Large

      pixelSize
        Font size in pixels. Must be greater than 0. This is the default for
        both singleByte and doubleByte.
      tracking
        Additional character spacing in pixels. May be positive, zero, or
        negative. Default: 0.
      fixedWidth
        Fixed logical cell width in pixels. 0 keeps proportional advances and
        is the default. A positive value centers each glyph body in a cell of
        this width, disables kerning and HarfBuzz shaping for that byte class,
        and makes its final advance fixedWidth + tracking. This follows the
        fixed-font behavior used by DCFGCF and is intended for grid-based UI
        such as the terminal hacking screen.
      scaleX / scaleY
        Horizontal and vertical glyph scale. Must be greater than 0.
        Default: 1.
      embolden
        FreeType outline emboldening in pixels. Must be 0 or greater.
        Default: 0. There is no weight attribute; use embolden or select a
        bold font file.
      slant
        Slant angle in degrees, clamped to the range -45 through 45.
        Positive values slant to the right. Default: 0. There is no italic
        attribute.
      baselineOffset
        Vertical baseline adjustment in pixels. May be positive or negative.
        Default: 0.
      verticalMetrics
        Selects the aggregate line-box source. "freetype" derives fBaseLine,
        fFontHeight, fMaxDrop, and space height from the configured faces and
        is the backward-compatible default. "original" preserves the line box
        loaded from the original .fnt while FreeType and HarfBuzz continue to
        position the actual glyphs. This attribute is read only from <font>.
      baseline
        Fallout fBaseLine (line rise/ascent) in pixels; it is not the total
        line-box height. In verticalMetrics="original" mode, 0 inherits the
        original .fnt baseline and a positive value takes priority over it.
        In verticalMetrics="freetype" mode, 0 derives it from both byte
        classes. Positive values round upward to the game's integer metric.
        Original mode retains automatic visual-center correction between byte
        classes even with a manual baseline. This attribute is read only from
        <font> and cannot be overridden per byte class. Default: 0.
      curveTolerance
        Curve-flattening tolerance for the vector fallback path. Minimum:
        0.01. Default: 0.35. The normal grayscale atlas path does not use it.
      shaping
        0 uses precise FreeType 26.6 advances and FT_Get_Kerning. 1 enables
        HarfBuzz GSUB/GPOS for ordinary Font text. Default: 0. Rich text keeps
        one CharData per encoded character and does not use GSUB substitutions.
      features
        Optional comma-separated HarfBuzz feature overrides, for example
        "kern,liga,clig,calt,ss01=1,-dlig". Unspecified features retain the
        HarfBuzz script defaults. Requires shaping="1". Invalid tokens reject
        the complete font ID configuration.
      prewarm
        Optional startup atlas prewarming mode. "none" disables prewarming
        and is the default. "common" incrementally prepares all valid
        single-byte units and up to 7000 valid double-byte units from the code
        page's standard common-character region. "codepage"
        incrementally prepares the explicit encoding ranges used by DCFGCF for
        the current uiEncoding code page. CP936 uses DCFGCF's complete GBK
        profile rather than its smaller GB2312 profile. Work is limited to a
        small batch per frame. Only raster scale 1.0 is prepared; UIO-derived
        sizes remain demand-driven. If the atlas reaches its maximum size,
        prewarming for that font stops safely.
      effectQuality
        Shader effect sampling preset: "fast", "balanced", or "high".
        Default: "balanced". Higher presets use more samples for smoother
        glow, outline, and blurred shadow edges. This attribute affects only
        the A8 PS 3.0 shader path; CPU fallback rendering remains available.
      fontColor
        Optional fill-color override in #RRGGBB format. When omitted, the
        renderer uses a white modifier and keeps the text color supplied by
        the game's Tile tint. XML RGB values multiply that Tile tint.
      fontAlpha
        Alpha for fontColor, clamped to the range 0 through 1. Default: 1.
        It is used only when fontColor is present.

      2. Single-byte and double-byte font groups

      <singleByte> handles valid single-byte encoding units in uiEncoding.
      <doubleByte> handles valid DBCS two-byte encoding units in uiEncoding.
      Shift-JIS half-width katakana use singleByte. Routing is based on the
      encoded byte length, not the Unicode range or glyph availability.

      singleByte and doubleByte may independently override these <font>
      attributes:
        pixelSize, tracking, fixedWidth, scaleX, scaleY, embolden, slant,
        baselineOffset

      Attributes not overridden by a byte class are inherited from <font>.
      shaping, features, verticalMetrics, baseline, curveTolerance, fontColor,
      fontAlpha, effectQuality, glow, outline, and shadow apply to the entire font ID and
      cannot be overridden per group.

      3. Font files and fallback chains

      <face path="..." index="0"/>
        Primary font. Every resolved byte class must have one primary face.
      <fallback path="..." index="0"/>
        Optional fallback font. Multiple fallbacks may be listed in lookup
        order.

      path
        May be absolute or relative to the FalloutNV.exe directory. Forward
        and backward slashes are accepted. Relative paths can be redirected
        through the MO2 virtual file system.
      index
        Face index inside the font collection. Default: 0. It must be 0 or
        greater. Regular TTF/OTF files normally use 0; TTC/OTC collections
        may use another index to select a contained face.

      A face/fallback chain directly under <font> is the default for both byte
      classes. A byte class with no face or fallback children inherits that
      chain. If a byte class contains any face or fallback child, its complete
      chain replaces the parent chain and it must provide a valid <face>.
      Glyph lookup never falls back across byte classes.

      4. Font effects

      <glow enabled="1" width="2" color="#FFFFFF" alpha="0.35"/>
      <outline enabled="1" width="1" color="#000000" alpha="1"/>
        enabled: 0 disables and 1 enables the effect. A present node defaults
                 to enabled when this attribute is omitted.
        width:   effect width in pixels. It must be greater than 0, otherwise
                 the effect is disabled.
        color:   #RRGGBB only. Default: #000000.
        alpha:   clamped to the range 0 through 1. Default: 1. An alpha of 0
                 disables the effect.

      <shadow enabled="1" x="1" y="1" blur="0" color="#000000" alpha="0.65"/>
        enabled, color, and alpha follow the same rules as above.
        x / y are shadow offsets in pixels, may be positive or negative, and
        default to 0.
        blur is an optional non-negative blur radius in pixels. Default: 0,
        which preserves the original hard shadow.

      An absent glow, outline, or shadow node disables that effect. Effect
      alpha and fontAlpha are multiplied by Tile c0 alpha supplied by the
      game. Effect RGB is multiplied by Tile c0 RGB. All custom FreeType
      shaders use this uniform-color contract and do not read COLOR0.
    -->

